{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Discovered while landing Gerrit change 1640 (ticket sole-curbable-stinkpot); it cost that\nchange a spurious Verified -1.\n\n## The defect\n\nTEST: tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck_keyset_prev_heldout.py::\ntest_cli_keyset_is_informational_and_keeps_json_envelope_read_only\n\nThe test proves `bridge fsck --keyset` does not mutate the tracker store. Its oracle is a\nbyte-for-byte snapshot of the tracker directory taken before and after the command:\n\n    full_before = _file_bytes(full_tracker)\n    ...\n    assert _file_bytes(full_tracker) == full_before\n\n`_file_bytes` walks the directory with `rglob(\"*\")` and excludes nothing, so `.git/` internals\nare part of the oracle. Git background auto-maintenance mutates those internals on its own\nschedule, independent of the command under test, so the comparison flips while the read-only\nproperty being asserted still holds. The failure diff observed in CI was a single stray entry\n`.git/objects/maintenance.lock`.\n\n## Root cause is broader than the observed symptom (measured, not assumed)\n\nInjecting the two things auto-maintenance does, against the current helper:\n\n- creating `.git/objects/maintenance.lock` -> snapshot comparison flips\n- `git gc` object packing -> snapshot comparison flips, 16 paths differ\n  (loose objects collapse into a packfile, `.git/info/refs` rewritten), with no logical change\n\nSo a fix that ignores only `*.lock` files would still leave the test flaky via the packing\nlimb. The oracle's problem is that it reaches into git's private storage representation at\nall, not that one particular scratch file exists.\n\n## The fix\n\nSnapshot what \"read-only\" actually claims, in two parts:\n\n1. Every file OUTSIDE `.git/` byte-for-byte (the store payload: event files, `.bridge_state/`,\n   bindings) - unchanged strictness for everything the store owns.\n2. Git's LOGICAL state rather than its storage bytes: `HEAD` plus the full `for-each-ref`\n   listing, so a commit or ref move made by the command is still caught.\n\nThis is deliberately not \"drop `.git/` and lose the check\". Repacking is invisible to (2)\nbecause packing does not move refs; a commit made by `bridge fsck` would move `HEAD` and fail\n(2) immediately. The oracle keeps its teeth and loses its dependence on git's scheduler.\n\nRejected alternative: `git config maintenance.auto false` / `gc.auto 0` in the fixture. It\nsuppresses this repo's symptom while leaving the oracle fragile to the next transient file, and\nit does not hold for maintenance already in flight or triggered by another process.\n\n## Scope\n\nTest-only change; no `src/rebar` behaviour is touched. The command under test is not modified,\nand the read-only property it is asserted against is not weakened.\n\nThe second flake recorded in the comments (test_plan_review_sidecar_emit_two_clone_union, a\nfixture-precondition sync race on ubuntu) is a DIFFERENT defect with a different mechanism and a\ndifferent fix. It is split into its own ticket and linked, rather than fixed here.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. A new regression test in the same file asserts the snapshot helper is stable across git\n   auto-maintenance: after injecting `.git/objects/maintenance.lock` AND running `git gc` in the\n   fixture tracker, the snapshot compares equal to the pre-injection snapshot. This test FAILS\n   against the current helper (verified: both limbs flip) and PASSES after the change.\n2. The same regression test asserts the oracle still detects real mutation: writing to a store\n   payload file makes the snapshot compare unequal, and a commit that moves `HEAD` makes it\n   compare unequal. Read-only detection is proven retained, not assumed.\n3. test_cli_keyset_is_informational_and_keeps_json_envelope_read_only still passes and still\n   asserts the read-only property via the corrected oracle.\n4. Assertions are ordering/content facts only - no wall-clock budgets or timing upper bounds.\n5. `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; the full bridge fsck test module passes."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786524189246836001,"uuid":"5d16ed74-f1c4-4fd7-885d-1950872b4bb3"}